  "articleBody": "Remodeling a bathroom is an exciting project, but skipping critical preparation can lead to costly delays and rework. Knowing the most frequent pitfalls helps you keep the renovation running smoothly and on schedule.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n\n- Ordering materials without verifying dimensions: Homeowners often purchase vanities, tub surrounds, or freestanding tubs before measuring door swings and plumbing rough-in centerlines. Verify rough plumbing distances and room clearances before purchasing.\n- Overlooking mechanical ventilation: Replacing finishes without upgrading an underpowered or improperly vented exhaust fan traps moisture, eventually ruining fresh drywall, paint, and grout.\n- Closing walls before rough inspections: Covering plumbing and electrical rough-ins with drywall or backer board before municipal inspectors approve the work can force contractors to tear down fresh installations.\n- Neglecting hidden moisture damage: Treating a remodel as purely surface work leads to trouble when old tile comes down and rotted studs or soft subfloors appear. Always reserve room in the project budget for structural repairs.\n- Using incorrect substrates in wet zones: Standard drywall or unsealed backer boards behind shower tile will deteriorate over time. Shower enclosures require dedicated cement backer boards paired with proven waterproofing systems.\n\nAvoiding these missteps requires thoughtful project planning. For hands-on help from an experienced contractor in the Denver area, call HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
